def put(s,d,t,ts,why,**kw):R[s]={'difficulty':d,'topic':t,'tags':ts.split(','),'reason':why,**kw}
put('pol_2023_1130d1',4.5,'Combinatorics','grid paths,extremal construction','The lower bound counts the rows and columns containing straight runs; an unvisited intersection appears if there are too few turns.',section='Second round, day 1 (10 February 2023)',statement=r'''A token starts in a corner cell of an $n\times n$ board, where $n\ge2$, initially facing into the board parallel to one side. It moves to an adjacent cell in its current direction, and before a move may turn left or right through $90^\circ$. What is the smallest number of turns needed to visit every cell at least once?''',solution=r'''The answer is $2n-2$. A row-by-row serpentine traversal achieves this, making two turns between successive rows.

For the lower bound, suppose there are at most $2n-3$ turns. The resulting straight runs alternate horizontal and vertical, so there are at most $n-1$ runs in each direction. All visited cells lie in the union of at most $n-1$ rows containing horizontal runs and at most $n-1$ columns containing vertical runs. Choose a row and a column absent from these lists. Their intersection cannot have been visited, a contradiction.''')
put('pol_2023_4abd98',4,'Algebra','inequalities,weighted variance','The double sum conceals a weighted variance. Expanding it reduces the result to a simple nonnegative average, but choosing that representation is the key step.',section='Final round, day 2 (30 March 2023)',statement=r'''Let $n\ge2$ and let positive real numbers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ have sum 1. Put $b=\sum_{i=1}^n ia_i$. Prove
$$\sum_{1\le i<j\le n}(j-i)^2a_ia_j\le(n-b)(b-1).$$''',solution=r'''Expanding the left side and using $\sum a_i=1$ gives
$$\sum_{i<j}(j-i)^2a_ia_j=\sum_i i^2a_i-b^2.$$
For each $1\le i\le n$, $(i-1)(n-i)\ge0$, or $i^2\le(n+1)i-n$. Multiply by $a_i$ and add to obtain $\sum i^2a_i\le(n+1)b-n$. Subtracting $b^2$ yields $(n-b)(b-1)$, as desired.''')
