put('pol_2023_90f4c3',5.5,'Combinatorics','queens,modular construction,finite fields','The successful construction consists of all nontrivial modular slopes; proving non-attacking and complete coverage requires using inverses modulo the prime.',section='Second round, day 2 (11 February 2023)',statement=r'''Let $n\ge4$ and suppose $p=n+1$ is prime. A set of $n$ cells of an $n\times n$ chessboard is called tactical if queens placed on these cells do not attack one another. Prove that $n-2$ tactical sets can cover every cell outside the board's two main diagonals.''',solution=r'''Label rows and columns by $1,\ldots,p-1$. For each $a\in\{2,\ldots,p-2\}$, take
$$X_a=\{(i,j):j\equiv ai\pmod p,\ 1\le i,j\le p-1\}.$$
There is one cell in each column, and because $a$ is invertible modulo $p$, one in each row. If two cells shared a rising or falling diagonal, then $a(i-i')\equiv\pm(i-i')\pmod p$. Since $i\ne i'$, this would force $a\equiv\pm1$, excluded. Thus $X_a$ is tactical.

For a cell $(i,j)$ outside the main diagonals, the residue $a\equiv ji^{-1}\pmod p$ is neither $1$ nor $-1$, so the cell belongs to the corresponding $X_a$. These $p-3=n-2$ sets suffice.''')
put('pol_2023_979ee2',6.5,'Combinatorics','rounding,pigeonhole principle,partial sums','The main insight is to round every partial sum using a common offset chosen from the largest gap between fractional parts; this controls every interval simultaneously.',section='Final round, day 1 (29 March 2023)',statement=r'''For $n\ge2$ let $a_1,\ldots,a_n\in[0,1]$. Prove that there are $b_1,\ldots,b_n\in\{0,1\}$ such that every $1\le k\le\ell\le n$ satisfies
$$\left|\sum_{i=k}^{\ell}(a_i-b_i)\right|\le\frac n{n+1}.$$''',solution=r'''Put $S_0=0$ and $S_j=a_1+\cdots+a_j$. On the unit circle, the at most $n+1$ fractional parts of $S_0,\ldots,S_n$ leave a gap of length at least $1/(n+1)$. Choose a rotation of the circle so that all these fractional parts lie in a closed interval of length at most $n/(n+1)$. Equivalently, there is a real number $c$ for which all remainders $\{S_j+c\}$ lie in such an interval.

Define $b_j=\lfloor S_j+c\rfloor-\lfloor S_{j-1}+c\rfloor$. Because $0\le S_j-S_{j-1}\le1$, each $b_j$ is 0 or 1. Finally
$$\sum_{j=k}^{\ell}(a_j-b_j)=\{S_\ell+c\}-\{S_{k-1}+c\},$$
and the right side has absolute value at most the length of the containing interval.''')
put('pol_2023_9c880b',5,'Geometry','incircle,orthocentre,cyclic quadrilaterals','The tangent-chord theorem first identifies one altitude of the auxiliary triangle; a second circle with a tangent segment as diameter gives the desired perpendicularity.',section='Second round, day 2 (11 February 2023)',statement=r'''In triangle $ABC$ with $AC<BC$, the incircle touches $AB$ and $AC$ at $D$ and $E$. The segment $CD$ meets the incircle again at $K$. Let $L$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $A$ to $CD$, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $DE$. If $H$ is the orthocentre of $KLM$, prove that $AH\perp HK$.''',solution=r'''Because $AD=AE$, the line $AM$ is perpendicular to $DE$. Thus $A,L,D,M$ lie on the circle with diameter $AD$. The tangent-chord theorem gives, in directed angles,
$$\angle KLM=\angle DAM=90^\circ-\angle MDA=90^\circ-\angle EKD.$$
Hence $LM\perp EK$, so the altitude through $K$ of triangle $KLM$ is $KE$, and $H\in KE$. Since $MH\perp KL$, another angle comparison gives
$$\angle MHE=90^\circ-\angle EKD=90^\circ-\angle AEM=\angle MAE.$$
Therefore $A,H,E,M$ are concyclic. Since $AM\perp EM$, this circle has diameter $AE$, and $AH\perp HE=HK$.''')
put('pol_2023_b04b9b',5.5,'Combinatorics','counting,Cauchy-Schwarz,symmetry','Splitting a 2n-term sum into two halves converts the comparison into a correlation bound; the hidden Cauchy-Schwarz step needs a suitable counting function.',section='Second round, day 1 (10 February 2023)',statement=r'''Let $a_1,\ldots,a_k$ be distinct positive reals and set $S=\{\pm a_1,\ldots,\pm a_k\}$. For a positive integer $n$ and real $t$, let $N(t)$ count ordered $2n$-tuples from $S$ whose sum is $t$. Prove that $N(0)\ge N(t)$ for every real $t$.''',solution=r'''Let $c(u)$ count ordered $n$-tuples from $S$ with sum $u$, setting it to zero for all other values. Only finitely many values are nonzero, and negating every entry gives $c(u)=c(-u)$. Splitting a tuple in half yields
$$N(0)=\sum_u c(u)^2,\qquad N(t)=\sum_u c(u)c(t-u).$$
The sums may be taken over the finite union of the supports involved. By Cauchy-Schwarz,
$$N(t)^2\le\left(\sum_u c(u)^2\right)\left(\sum_u c(t-u)^2\right)=N(0)^2.$$
Both counts are nonnegative, proving the claim.''')
put('pol_2023_d94e4c',4.5,'Algebra','cyclic systems,factorisation','Multiplying the four shifted-factor equations recovers a missing fifth relation; multiplying the resulting difference identities telescopes to the product.',section='Second round, day 2 (11 February 2023)',statement=r'''Distinct real numbers $a,b,c,d,e$ satisfy
$$ab+b=ac+a,\quad bc+c=bd+b,\quad cd+d=ce+c,\quad de+e=da+d.$$
Prove that $abcde=1$.''',solution=r'''Rewrite the equations as $b(a+1)=a(c+1)$, $c(b+1)=b(d+1)$, $d(c+1)=c(e+1)$ and $e(d+1)=d(a+1)$. Distinctness implies $b,c,d\ne0$ and $a,c,d\ne-1$: a zero or a forbidden -1 would force two adjacent displayed variables equal. Multiplying and cancelling these nonzero factors yields the missing relation $e(b+1)=a(e+1)$.

The five equations now read
$$a(b-c)=a-b,\ b(c-d)=b-c,\ c(d-e)=c-d,\ d(e-a)=d-e,\ e(a-b)=e-a.$$
Multiply them and cancel the nonzero product $(a-b)(b-c)(c-d)(d-e)(e-a)$. The result is $abcde=1$.''')
put('pol_2023_dd96c4',7,'Geometry','arc midpoint,equilateral triangle,power of a point','The useful auxiliary equilateral triangle is far from immediate; two circle-power identities then produce the similarity that splits the 120-degree angle.',section='Final round, day 1 (29 March 2023)',statement=r'''Let $I$ be the incentre of acute triangle $ABC$. A point $X$ lies on $BC$, on the same side of $AI$ as $B$, and $Y$ lies on the minor arc $AB$ of the circumcircle. Suppose $\angle AIX=\angle XYA=120^\circ$. Prove that $YI$ bisects $\angle XYA$.''',solution=r'''Let $M$ be the midpoint of the minor arc $BC$. The arc-midpoint lemma gives $MB=MC=MI$. Construct equilateral triangle $IMZ$ on the side of $IM$ containing $X$. Then $I,X,Z$ are collinear. Put $T=XY\cap MZ$. Since $\angle XYA=120^\circ$ and the angle between $MA$ and $MZ$ is $60^\circ$, the cyclic angle criterion shows that $T$ lies on the circumcircle of $ABC$ (directed angles cover either intersection order).

The points $B,C,I,Z$ lie on the circle with centre $M$. Applying the power-of-a-point theorem at $X$ to this circle and to the circumcircle of $ABC$ gives
$$XI\cdot XZ=XB\cdot XC=XY\cdot XT.$$
Thus $XI/XY=XT/XZ$, and the corresponding included angles at $X$ agree. Triangles $IXY$ and $TXZ$ are similar, so $\angle XYI=\angle TZI=60^\circ$. Since $\angle XYA=120^\circ$, the claim follows.''')
