put('rus_2025_8671a6',4.5,'Combinatorics','strategy games,invariants,pairing','Selecting ten spaced entries and pairing them turns every opposing move into a loss that a single response can restore in pair-sums.',solution=r'''Track the ten positions $9,18,27,36,45,54,63,72,90,99$, grouped as $(9,18),(27,36),(45,54),(63,72),(90,99)$. Every cyclic gap between consecutive tracked positions is at least 9, so a block of nine consecutive positions contains at most one tracked position. Whenever Petya decreases a tracked entry, Vasya increases the block of ten consecutive entries whose endpoints are that entry's pair. The sum of that pair loses 2 and then gains 2; every other pair can only increase. If no tracked entry is decreased, Vasya may make any legal move. All five pair-sums therefore remain at least their initial value 2. Each pair contains a positive entry, giving at least five positive entries after every response.''',notes='The source proof has a subscript typo; the response is stated with explicit pairs.')
put('rus_2025_868220',6.5,'Number Theory','perfect squares,gcd of powers,highest power of two','The case n=3 needs coprime-factor reasoning; the general obstruction isolates a nonsquare factor at the highest power-of-two index.',solution=r'''The possible values are $n=1,2$: use $a=2$ and $a=8$, respectively.

For $n=3$, write the product as $(a-1)^3(a+1)(a^2+a+1)$. Since $a$ is even, $a+1$ is coprime to both other factors, so $a+1$ is a square. Modulo 3 this implies $a-1\not\equiv0$. Hence $\gcd(a-1,a^2+a+1)=\gcd(a-1,3)=1$, so $a^2+a+1$ must be a square. But it lies strictly between $a^2$ and $(a+1)^2$.

For $n\ge4$, choose $k\ge2$ with $2^k\le n<2^{k+1}$. Split the factor at index $2^k$ as
$$a^{2^k}-1=(a^{2^{k-1}}-1)(a^{2^{k-1}}+1).$$
Let $F=a^{2^{k-1}}+1$. It is coprime to the first factor because both are odd and differ by 2. For every other index $m\le n$, $2^k\nmid m$. The identity $\gcd(a^u-1,a^v-1)=a^{\gcd(u,v)}-1$ shows that any common divisor of $F$ and $a^m-1$ also divides $a^{2^{k-1}}-1$, so is 1. Thus $F$ is coprime to the remaining product and must itself be a square. But $a^{2^{k-1}}$ is already a positive square, and its successor is not. This contradiction finishes the proof.''',notes='Several misplaced exponents in the transcription are corrected in this exposition.')
